First Author: Ahady Dolatsara
Title: A two-stage machine learning framework to predict heart transplantation survival probabilities over time with a monotonic probability constraint
Source number: 3  ￼

Very short summary: This study proposed a two-stage machine-learning framework to predict personalized post-heart-transplant survival probabilities over multiple time points and then enforce monotonicity using isotonic regression. Five key findings were: (1) the study used a national U.S. UNOS heart transplant dataset covering 1987–2016; (2) Stage I evaluated multiple ML approaches, with the reported selected implementation being UP-LASSO-logistic regression; (3) holdout AUCs increased over time from 0.608 at month 1 to about 0.70 at years 8–10; (4) isotonic regression improved AUC slightly at all time points and guaranteed monotonic survival curves; and (5) the paper made both code and a web app publicly available. Figures showed the two-stage framework, transplant decision process, ROC curves, monotonic probability correction, and observed-vs-forecast calibration plots; Tables 4 and 5 reported holdout performance before and after isotonic regression.  ￼  ￼  ￼

