	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: Missing data were handled through a structured cleaning strategy rather than a single standard method: variables with high missingness were removed, and the authors compared models with no imputation versus median/mode imputation; categorical missingness could also be imputed as “unknown.” Class imbalance was explicitly addressed with several subsampling methods: “none, DOWN, UP, SMOTE and ROSE.” Data preprocessing was described in detail, including removal of intra/post-transplant variables, censoring rules, engineered features, regrouping categorical levels, and encoding choices. Code availability was stronger than a simple yes/no because the paper states that “our code, analysis and results are detailed in an R Markdown document” and also provides a public web app; however, algorithm weights were not reported. Exact training/test N for the selected final model at each time point were not provided in the retrieved text.  ￼  ￼

