Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a large national UNOS registry with 103,570 heart transplant events, which lowers single-center selection bias, although censoring varied by time point.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The authors intentionally removed intra- and post-transplant variables in the main prediction framework, keeping a pre-operative decision-support orientation, and also engineered clinically motivated donor-recipient features.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The survival outcome at each time point was explicitly coded using G-TIME and G-STATUS, with censoring rules clearly described for whether the recipient was alive or dead at the time point of interest.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include extensive preprocessing, class-imbalance handling, multiple algorithms, cross-validation, holdout testing, and post hoc monotonic calibration; however, the very large analytic design space and time-point-specific censoring introduce complexity and possible instability, and exact final training/test sample sizes per horizon were not clearly reported in the retrieved text.  ￼  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This article directly concerns AI/ML for heart transplant outcome prediction using donor-recipient pre-operative variables and is highly aligned with the review question.  ￼

